          <p>
            A word on method, because it matters for how much weight to put on this list. We did not run a laboratory or a taste panel. These rankings come from reading labels, manufacturer data and certification listings, and from published nutrition research, using the criteria laid out in <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test</a>. Where we mention taste or mixability, we are reporting what buyers widely say, not what we measured. Label analysis cannot tell you how a chocolate flavor tastes, but it can tell you exactly how much you are paying for protein and how much for filler.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more thing worth saying before the product cards: protein powder is a convenience food. It is milk or legume protein dried into a shelf-stable form, and it does nothing that the same amount of protein from chicken, eggs, yogurt or lentils would not also do. Its value is that it is fast, portable, easy to measure and usually cheap per gram. If you already eat enough protein, the best protein powder for you may be no protein powder at all, and we explain who falls into that group in the final section.
          </p>
