        <div id="choose-the-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Whey, Isolate or Plant: Choose the Type Before the Brand
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every powder on the shelf is one of four things: whey concentrate, whey isolate, a plant blend, or casein. The type decides most of what you will notice, from digestion to price, far more than the brand does. Whey concentrate is typically 70 to 80% protein by weight and carries a few grams of lactose and fat per serving. Isolate is filtered further, usually to 90% protein or more, with lactose often under a gram. Many products sold simply as whey, including our top pick, are blends of the two. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> comparison walks through the filtering in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Isolate is worth its higher price in three situations: you are lactose sensitive, you are in a calorie deficit and every gram of carbohydrate and fat counts, or you want the most protein per scoop. It is not more muscle-building gram for gram. Once the protein dose is matched, research has not shown isolate producing meaningfully better gains than concentrate, because both are the same whey proteins with different amounts of lactose and fat attached. If isolate is your lane, our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> guide ranks the field in more depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant proteins, usually pea, rice, soy or a blend, are the answer for vegans and people with dairy allergies. Individually they tend to be lower in leucine, the amino acid most closely tied to switching on muscle protein synthesis, and some are slightly less digestible. Blends and larger servings close most of that gap: a frequently cited French trial comparing pea and whey protein over a 12-week training program found similar gains in muscle thickness. The <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein</a> breakdown covers the trade-offs, and our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> roundup ranks the vegan options. One caution worth knowing early: plant proteins have, on average, tested higher for heavy metals than dairy proteins, which we cover in the safety section.
          </p>

          <p>
            Casein is the other milk protein. It clots in the stomach and releases amino acids over several hours instead of the quick spike you get from whey, which is why it is marketed for nighttime. It is a perfectly good protein at any time of day and makes a thick, filling shake, but nobody needs it. If your daily total is covered, whey or food will do the same work.
          </p>

