        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How We Picked: Five Things We Read on Every Label
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every product here was judged on the same five label criteria, and every one is something you can check yourself on any tub in about a minute. None of them involves the front of the package, which is where marketing lives. The first is <strong>protein per serving</strong>. We want at least 20 g, and ideally 24 to 30 g, because that is the range that delivers enough leucine to maximize the muscle-building response from a single dose in most adults. A powder that gives you 15 g per scoop forces you to use two scoops and doubles your real cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second, and the one most buyers skip, is <strong>protein as a percentage of scoop weight</strong>. Divide the grams of protein by the serving size in grams. A good whey blend lands around 75 to 80%, an isolate 85 to 90%, and a decent plant blend 65 to 75%. Anything far below that is mostly something else: sugar, fiber, gums, creamers, or flavoring. Organic all-in-one shakes are the usual offenders. Orgain Organic Protein, for example, lists about 21 g of protein in a 46 g serving on its label at the time of writing, which is under 50% protein. That is not a scandal, but it does mean you are buying a flavored drink mix that contains protein, not a protein powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third is <strong>cost per 25 g of protein</strong>, which is the only honest way to compare tubs of different sizes, scoop weights and protein densities. Take the price, divide it by the total grams of protein in the tub (servings times protein per serving), and multiply by 25. Run that sum and the big tub with the low sticker price is often not the cheapest, and a mid-priced tub with a dense scoop often is. We group products into budget, mid and premium tiers at typical retail prices rather than quoting dollar figures that change weekly.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fourth is <strong>independent certification</strong>: NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Choice or Informed Sport. These programs screen for banned substances and check the product against its label, and they are the closest thing the supplement industry has to an outside audit. A brand saying its product is lab tested is not the same thing, because the brand chose the lab and the brand decides what to publish. The fifth is the <strong>ingredient list</strong> itself: the shorter it is, and the closer protein sits to the top, the better. We penalized proprietary blends, long lists of added free amino acids, and fillers that dilute the scoop.
          </p>

